imedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-500px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Formation of non–bead-cultured Akoya \"keshi\" pearls produced in a P. i. fucata mollusk. (A) Optical overview of a nonbeaded keshi–cultured pearl. (B) Cross-section showing CaCO3 growth begins onto an organic center. (C) Mature nacre. (D and E) Atomic-resolution image of atoms in nacre. (F) Transition from spherulitic aragonite structures to nacre. (G and H) Aggregated nanoparticles form massive aragonite. (I) The formation of nacre begins directly on massive aragonite.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg/500px-Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg/500px-Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two halved river pearls, natural freshwater pearls, on a slab of lapis lazuli. The pearls are about 2.5–3 mm (0.098–0.118 in) in diameter. The growth layers are clearly visible.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwo_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Pearls.jpg/500px-Pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Pearls.jpg/500px-Pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nuclei from Mikimoto Pear